{%- comment -%}
  Rating line, read from Judge.me.

  Judge.me writes two standard Shopify metafields on every product it has
  reviews for:
    product.metafields.reviews.rating        type `rating`  -> { rating, scale_min, scale_max }
    product.metafields.reviews.rating_count  type `number_integer`
  Those are the single source of truth. This snippet computes nothing of its
  own and stores nothing of its own, so the number in the buy box, the number
  on a card and the number in Judge.me's own widget can never disagree.

  It replaces a version that averaged a `review` metaobject maintained inside
  this theme. That metaobject is empty and Judge.me does not write to it, so
  every star row on the site was rendering nothing while 20 real reviews sat
  in Judge.me unused. Two review systems is one too many; this is the one that
  has the data.

  Usage:
    {% render 'product-rating', product: product %}                  <- a card
    {% render 'product-rating', scope: 'store', verbose: true %}     <- a grid head

  Params:
    product   required for the default scope
    scope     'product' (default) reads this product's own metafields and
              renders nothing when it has no reviews.
              'store' aggregates across the catalogue, count-weighted, and
              ignores `product`.
    verbose   true writes "20 reviews" instead of "(20)" (default false)
    class     extra class on the wrapper (default none)

  Never invents a rating: with no reviews in scope the whole block is absent,
  not a row of empty stars implying a score of nothing.
{%- endcomment -%}
{%- liquid
  assign want = scope | default: 'product'
  assign avg = 0
  assign n = 0

  if want == 'store'
    comment
      Count-weighted, not a mean of means: a product with 200 reviews and one
      with 2 do not carry the same weight, and averaging the two averages would
      say they do. Capped at 50 products, which is Liquid's default page size —
      past that this needs Judge.me's own shop-level widget rather than a
      bigger loop on every page render.
    endcomment
    assign total = 0
    for p in collections.all.products limit: 50
      assign c = p.metafields.reviews.rating_count.value | default: 0
      if c > 0
        assign total = total | plus: p.metafields.reviews.rating.value.rating | times: c
        assign n = n | plus: c
      endif
    endfor
    if n > 0
      assign avg = total | divided_by: n | round: 1
    endif
  else
    assign n = product.metafields.reviews.rating_count.value | default: 0
    if n > 0
      assign avg = product.metafields.reviews.rating.value.rating | times: 1.0 | round: 1
    endif
  endif
-%}
{%- if n > 0 and avg > 0 -%}
  {%- liquid
    comment
      The fill width is derived from the same rounded average that is printed
      beside it, so the stars and the numeral can never disagree. Scaled off
      the metafield's own scale_max rather than a hardcoded 5, because a rating
      metafield carries its scale with it.
    endcomment
    assign scale = product.metafields.reviews.rating.value.scale_max | default: 5
    if want == 'store' or scale == 0
      assign scale = 5
    endif
    assign pct = avg | times: 100.0 | divided_by: scale
  -%}
  <p class="pc-rating {{ class }}">
    <span class="pc-rating-stars" role="img" aria-label="Rated {{ avg }} out of {{ scale }} from {{ n }} review{% if n != 1 %}s{% endif %}">
      <span class="pc-rating-track" aria-hidden="true">★★★★★</span>
      <span class="pc-rating-fill" aria-hidden="true" style="width:{{ pct }}%">★★★★★</span>
    </span>
    <span class="pc-rating-avg" aria-hidden="true">{{ avg }}</span>
    <span class="pc-rating-count" aria-hidden="true">
      {%- if verbose -%}{{ n }} review{% if n != 1 %}s{% endif %}{%- else -%}({{ n }}){%- endif -%}
    </span>
  </p>
{%- endif -%}
